<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "The for Loop". GNU FDL 1.3; see ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-for-loop">
  <title>The <c>for</c> Loop</title>

  <p>
    The most common loop in JavaScript is the <c>for</c> loop.
    Here it is again:
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="try-for-loop-1">
    <title>Your First for Loop</title>
    <program xml:id="ac-for-loop-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
    <code>
for (let i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
  console.log("Practice makes perfect");
}
    </code>
  </program>
  </activity>

  <p>
    A <c>for</c> loop has two main parts: a <term>header</term> and a <term>body</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <term>loop body</term> is the code block between the curly braces <c>{ }</c>.
    It is the code that gets repeated.
    In this loop, the body is one line: <c>console.log("Practice makes perfect");</c>.
    A body can have as many lines as you need.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <term>loop header</term> is the first line, inside the parentheses.
    It controls how many times the body runs.
    It has three parts, split up by semicolons:
  </p>

  <program language="javascript">
    <code>
for (start; condition; update) {
  // body
}
    </code>
  </program>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        <term>Start</term>: <c>let i = 0</c>.
        This makes a new variable named <c>i</c> and sets it to <c>0</c>.
        This variable is called the <term>loop variable</term>.
        The start runs only once, before anything else.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <term>Condition</term>: <c>i &lt; 5</c>.
        This is checked before every pass.
        If it is <c>true</c>, the body runs.
        If it is <c>false</c>, the loop stops.
        It works just like the condition in an <c>if</c> statement:
        it is a comparison that comes out <c>true</c> or <c>false</c>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <term>Update</term>: <c>i++</c>.
        This runs after every pass through the body.
        <c>i++</c> means <q>add 1 to <c>i</c></q>.
        It is a short way to write <c>i = i + 1</c>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    So you can read the loop above out loud like this:
    <q>Start <c>i</c> at 0. As long as <c>i</c> is less than 5, print the message, then add 1 to <c>i</c>.</q>
  </p>

  <p>
    The loop variable is a real variable, so you can use it in the body.
    Run this program and look at the output.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="try-for-loop-2">
    <title>Watching the Loop Variable</title>
    <program xml:id="ac-for-loop-2" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
    <code>
for (let i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
  console.log("i is now " + i);
}
    </code>
  </program>
  </activity>

  <p>
    The body ran five times, and each time <c>i</c> held a new number: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.
    It never printed 5. When <c>i</c> became 5, the condition <c>5 &lt; 5</c> was <c>false</c>, so the loop stopped before the body could run.
  </p>

  <p>
    The name <c>i</c> is a habit programmers have used for a very long time.
    It is short for <q>index</q>.
    You can name the loop variable anything you like, but <c>i</c> is what you will see in most code, and on most tests.
  </p>

  <note>
    <title>Syntax rules</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>The header goes inside parentheses <c>( )</c>.</p></li>
      <li><p>The three parts are split up by two semicolons <c>;</c>. There is no semicolon after the update.</p></li>
      <li><p>The body goes inside curly braces <c>{ }</c>. Indent the body so it is easy to see what is inside the loop.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use <c>let</c> for the loop variable, not <c>const</c>, because the update changes it.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </note>

  <note>
    <title>On the test you will see <c>var</c></title>
    <p>
      This book writes <c>for (let i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)</c>.
      Operation Spark's tests write <c>for (var i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)</c>.
      <c>var</c> is the old way to make a variable.
      In the loops on the test, it works the same as <c>let</c>, so read <c>var</c> as if it said <c>let</c>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p><alert>Check your understanding</alert></p>

  <exercise xml:id="mc-for-loop-body" label="mc-for-loop-body">
    <statement>
      <p>Which part of a <c>for</c> loop is the code that gets repeated?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>The body, inside the curly braces</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Right. Everything inside the curly braces runs once per pass.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The start</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. The start runs only once, before the loop begins.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The condition</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. The condition decides <em>whether</em> to run the code again. It is not the code that is repeated.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The whole header, inside the parentheses</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. The header controls the loop. The repeated code is somewhere else.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="mc-for-loop-start-once" label="mc-for-loop-start-once">
    <statement>
      <p>How many times does the start part of a <c>for</c> loop run?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Once, before the first pass</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Right. If the start ran every pass, the loop variable would reset each time and the loop would never end.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Once before every pass</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. That is the condition. The start only sets the loop variable up.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Once after every pass</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. That is the update.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="mc-for-loop-update" label="mc-for-loop-update">
    <statement>
      <p>What does <c>i++</c> do?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Adds 1 to <c>i</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Right. It is the same as <c>i = i + 1</c>.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Adds 2 to <c>i</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. Two plus signs does not mean <q>add 2</q>.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Checks if <c>i</c> is a positive number</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. <c>i++</c> changes <c>i</c>. It does not check anything.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Prints <c>i</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. Only <c>console.log</c> prints.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="parsons-for-loop-hello" label="parsons-for-loop-hello">
    <statement>
      <p>Put the blocks in order to make a loop that prints <c>"Hello"</c> ten times.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
      <block><cline>for (let i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {</cline></block>
      <block><cline>  console.log("Hello");</cline></block>
      <block><cline>}</cline></block>
      <block correct="no"><cline>for (let i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++);</cline></block>
      <block correct="no"><cline>for (let i = 0, i &lt; 10, i++) {</cline></block>
    </blocks>
  </exercise>
</section>
